<p>Academia is in turmoil regarding partially or wholly AI-written research. AI-written work is being submitted to journals, but it isn&#8217;t clear how much. Publisher, journal, university, and department policies are emerging and fluctuating. Technology develops, rendering recently enacted policies ambiguous, vague, or subject to unintended loopholes. <span id="more-58188"></span></p>
<p>With everything in flux, it&#8217;s impossible for norms to develop.</p>
<p>There is no general sense of whether and what kind and what degree of AI use by authors is acceptable. Different policies across different institutions means that what may be problematic AI use in one place is acceptable AI use in another, and vice versa.</p>
<p>Into this chaos steps forward <em>the vigilantes</em>. The vigilantes have always been there, but now they are newly armed and emboldened. In the everchanging and never ending race between the technology of deployment and the technology of detection, the latter seems to have a temporary lead in the form of <a href="https://www.pangram.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pangram</a>. You can feed a text into Pangram and it will tell you its analysis of whether it is AI generated, and if so, what percentage of it is AI generated.</p>
<p>Pangram claims to have a remarkably high detection and low false-positive rates, and cites studies by third parties to support these claims. Yet I have seen plenty of posts by friends and colleagues across social media showing false positives with some of their own work. So user beware.</p>
<p>That said, even if Pangram&#8217;s claims about its accuracy are correct, it won&#8217;t always be helpful to simply know <em>that</em> a text is &#8220;AI generated.&#8221; This is because there are two further problems.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class=" wp-image-58207 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/ai-vigilante-1024x725.png" alt="" width="706" height="500" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/ai-vigilante-1024x725.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/ai-vigilante-300x213.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/ai-vigilante-768x544.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/ai-vigilante-400x283.png 400w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/ai-vigilante.png 1200w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 706px) 100vw, 706px" /></p>
<p>The first problem is ambiguity about what <em>&#8220;N%</em> AI generated&#8221; means. If someone asks Claude to write them a novel article about a philosophical problem, not even giving it a thesis to work with but asking it to come up with one itself, and that&#8217;s the extent of the human&#8217;s contribution, the resultant paper will get a &#8220;100% AI generated&#8221; evaluation. And if someone spends a year researching, writing, workshopping, and revising a paper, and then asks an AI to improve the writing style throughout the paper, the resultant paper will get a &#8220;100% AI generated&#8221; evaluation, too. Despite their same scores, though, these seem like cases that it might be plausible to treat quite differently.  And of course, there are countless variations of AI-use between these extremes.</p>
<p>The issue remains even for &#8220;0% AI generated&#8221; findings, for such a score might be given to both an article that was written entirely by a human who never used any AI tools, and an article written entirely by an AI after minimal prompting by a human, but then rewritten by the human in their own words and style.</p>
<p>The second problem concerns the variation in policies across academic publishers and journals. Different kinds of AI use are allowed or prohibited in different venues. Learning that Pangram reports a published article as, say, &#8220;50% AI generated&#8221;, even if that figure is in some sense correct, cannot tell us whether the AI use it&#8217;s detecting was illicit, except if the article is published somewhere that prohibits any and all AI use. Not only does that percentage not tell us <em>how </em>the AI was used, but it doesn&#8217;t tell us whether the AI use violated any of the journal&#8217;s or publisher&#8217;s policies.</p>
<p>Now it is still the case, today, that most philosophers disapprove of other philosophers using AI to write their articles. Whether you agree or not, many philosophers view the use of AI to write for you as wrong&#8212;as a form of plagiarism or cheating, or a misunderstanding of what it is to do philosophy. In short, &#8220;AI generated&#8221; is, to most ears in our discipline, a kind of condemnation.</p>
<p>What this means is that when someone feeds an article into Pangram and publicly reports that it was &#8220;100% AI generated&#8221;, they are accusing the named author(s) of professional misconduct.</p>
<p>That is a serious accusation! It can be quite damaging. Given how human psychology works (and how internet searches work), even if the author is shown to not have acted wrongly, negative connotations may linger, damaging the author&#8217;s professional prospects for years to come.</p>
<p>Given the possible damage, and given the two problems described above, it seems we have strong reasons, at least for now, to refrain from publicly posting the &#8220;scores&#8221; of articles fed into AI detection services like Pangram.</p>
<p>What&#8217;s an alternative? Well, you could initially <em>privately</em> contact the author, the editor, or the publisher with your concerns.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong><span style="color: #808080;">*</span> * <span style="color: #808080;">*</span></strong></p>
<p>As some readers will have figured out, the foregoing was motivated by a recent actual case in which one philosopher posted on social media that another philosopher&#8217;s recently published article was, according to Pangram, 100% AI generated, despite the article lacking an acknowledgment of AI use. This led to an investigation by the publisher (Open Library of Humanities), which ended up clearing the author of any wrongdoing. The publisher wrote:</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>The investigation found no issues with the content of the article that would suggest any academic malpractice was committed, nor does the OLH have any reason to believe that generative AI was used to the extent that the complainant suggested, or with the intent to commit academic malpractice&#8230; In line with the OLH’s ‘Artificial Intelligence (AI) Policy’ on providing an ‘AI Declaration Statement’, the author did not meet the criteria set out within the policy to provide a public declaration statement, namely: ‘the use of generative AI that forms the basis of a specific research purpose’, ‘the use of generative AI that forms the basis of a controlled simulation that produces outputs, such as synthetic data to supplement partial datasets, which informs the article&#8217;s argument(s)’, or ‘the use of generative AI for specific demonstrative purposes, with generated output(s) quoted and cited clearly with the prompts used, the model version and the URL of the service as part of its citation or caption’&#8230;</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>[W]e have concluded that there are no verifiable issues found that provide or support accusations of AI misuse or malpractice. </em></p>
<p>You can learn more about this case <a href="https://politicalphilosophyjournal.org/news/985/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>. Note: I ask that readers not use or mention the name of the author or the title of the article in that case in the comments here. The article was published in <em>Political Philosophy, </em>so if need be you can call it the PP case.</p>
<p>By the way, the philosopher who had posted on social media about the article’s Pangram score has now kindly removed his original post about it.</p>
