<p style="text-align: center;"><strong><span style="color: #808080;">*</span> * <span style="color: #808080;">*</span></strong></p>
<p>Relatedly, a friend recently wrote in with a tip for referees concerned about refereeing AI-written work: start with the references.</p>
<p>They write:</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>Usually, before reading a paper, I read the references to get a sense of the context of the paper. I immediately noticed a reference to me that was fabricated: I have no paper with that title and have never published on that topic in the journal it was supposedly in. When I put the fabricated reference into Google AI and then Chatgpt (free versions), both responded by saying it was a paper in [journal name redacted]&#8230; Only after I responded, &#8220;this paper does not exist!&#8221; did they both reply with their usual, &#8220;sorry, I made a mistake,&#8221; etc.</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>I then checked the rest of the references in the paper and found 3 other errors: 2 relatively minor and another fabrication. </em></p>
<p>Other suggestions welcome. Use them responsibly.</p><p>The post <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/08/27/careful-with-those-accusations/">Careful with those Accusations</a> first appeared on <a href="https://dailynous.com">Daily Nous</a>.</p>